speechtext:
My hon. friend is putting to me questions of a character that could not possibly be answered in the terms which the questions indicate he desires. I am stating my own opinions and I have no hesitancy in doing so. I was going to direct his attention to section 4 of the Tariff Board Act:
In respect of goods produced in or imported into Canada the Board shall, at the request of the minister, make inquiry as to-
(a)	the price and cost of raw materials in Canada and elsewhere, and the cost of transportation thereof from the place of production to the place of use or consumption;
(b)	the cost of efficient production in Canada and elsewhere, and what increases or decreases in rates of duty are required to equalize differences in the cost of efficient production;
(c)	the cost, efficiency and conditions of labour, including health of employees, in Canada and elsewhere;
(d)	the prices received by producers, manufacturers, wholesale dealers, retailers and other distributors in Canada and elsewhere;
(e)	all conditions and factors which affect or enter into the cost of production and the price to the consumers in Canada;
(f)	generally, all the conditions affecting production, manufacture, cost and price in Canada as compared with other countries;
And so on.
In other rvords, the board will consider the well-recognized principles and factors that enter into the cost of production. So far as I am concerned, and I again make it clear that I am stating my own views, just as my hon. friends have done, I do not think that approval should be given to what is known as the watering of stock. I think that is a practice which is bound to bring disaster and should be frowned upon.
1336	COMMONS
Imperial Conference-Trade Agreements
